  "content": {
    "id": "p2-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Consider the following sequence whose rule is\n$$a_n=n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)+1.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p2-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the first four terms of this sequence and show that each of these terms is a perfect square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$25=5^2,\\ 121=11^2,\\ 361=19^2,\\ 841=29^2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a_1&=(1\\times2\\times3\\times4)+1=25=5^2,\\\\a_2&=(2\\times3\\times4\\times5)+1=121=11^2,\\\\a_3&=(3\\times4\\times5\\times6)+1=361=19^2,\\\\a_4&=(4\\times5\\times6\\times7)+1=841=29^2.\\end{align*}$$\nEach term is the square of an integer.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p2-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that every term in this sequence is a perfect square. Hint: Expand then factorise.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a_n=(n^2+3n+1)^2$.\n\nExpand and factorise; $n^2+3n+1$ is an integer for every positive integer $n$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a_n&=n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)+1\\\\a_n&=n^4+6n^3+11n^2+6n+1\\\\a_n&=(n^2+3n+1)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nFor every positive integer $n$, $n^2+3n+1$ is an integer. Hence every term is a perfect square.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
